A||||2||lennyb4||Interior Repairs..||||14||
Z||000000||lennyb4||07-06-2000||03:34 PM||||I know many (and i do mean many) of us have the problem of the various corners of our interiors peeling and curling up. Well i tried four or five different types of glue's with no luck. Then i found it. It's called "Hot Stuff - Super "T" glue. Its approx. 10x stronger then super glues and adhesives and sets in under a minute (just long enough to line the edge's up) and completely cures in under 5. I got it at a woodworking / hobby store for about $15.00 a bottle (good size bottle to, i'll probally never use all of it). They also sell a instant curing agent for it, BUT i suggest you dont use it. It will cure on contact and makes it kinda hard to work with. I just wanted to let you guys and gals know about this stuff. I've done my radio pod, center console, and my rear glove box in about two hours and they all look new. (and a special thanks to who ever had the paint stick idea, it worked great).<P> P.S.<BR> If you use this stuff be sure to get it right the first time. Dry fit and trim as necessary to a good fit. There is NO known release agent for it.||||reg||14
Z||000001||286SE'S||07-06-2000||03:56 PM||||I am suprized you were not sucessfull with regular SuperGlue. I did my shift plate and radio surround 4 years ago and they are still tight and look great. My car sits in hot California sun every day. (no garage). <BR>The glue is important but cleaning the surfaces is critical. You must get all the old yellow foam off of the vinyl and the plastic, scrub clean with alcohol and tightly clamp the parts.<BR>Terry||||reg||1
